    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-026-07230-2",
    "abstract": "The Southwestern South Atlantic (SWSA) is a key region for climate research and renewable energy assessment, yet high-resolution meteorological data are scarce. We present a multiresolution dataset spanning February 2017-November 2018, combining Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) simulations with Sentinel-1A/B Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) wind fields processed using the CMOD5 model. WRF outputs were generated every 30 minutes for three nested domains (9 km, 3 km, 1 km) through 325 short-term simulations. SAR/CMOD5 wind fields are provided at 500 m and 1 km resolution across 104 acquisition dates. Validation shows strong agreement: daily spatial averages of 10 m wind speed yield RMSE and MAE below 3 m/s on over 93% of acquisition days, while more than 91.5% of pixel-level deviations fall within \u00b13 m/s. In situ measurements from the Itaja\u00ed buoy further confirmed the reliability of both sources. The dataset supports regional climate studies, wind energy resource assessment, and machine-learning applications in forecasting and downscaling, with usage examples included to aid practical adoption.",
    "field": "Regional climate and weather modeling",
    "problem": "High-resolution meteorological data for the Southwestern South Atlantic are scarce, limiting climate research and renewable energy assessment, as existing global datasets have insufficient resolution to accurately represent important small-scale atmospheric phenomena.",
    "method": "We present a multiresolution weather dataset for the Southwestern South Atlantic by combining Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model simulations with wind fields derived from Sentinel-1 Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) satellite data to address the scarcity of high-resolution meteorological information in the region.",
    "task": "The task is to create a multiresolution weather dataset for the Southwestern South Atlantic by combining Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model simulations with Sentinel-1 Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) wind fields to address the scarcity of high-resolution meteorological data in the region.",
