            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Sentinel-1 SAR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "CMOD5 algorithm",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "SAR images from Sentinel-1A/B C-band sensors [15, 16] were processed with the CMOD5 algorithm to estimate 10 m wind fields.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "RMSE of 1.795 m/s",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The cumulative distributions of the absolute deviations between the SAR/CMOD5 and WRF models are shown in Figure 5b. The deviation distribution is concentrated at low values: 44.7% of the absolute deviations are below 1 m/s, more than 75% are below 2m/s, and over 90% remain under 3m/s. Eror metrics [58] indicate a RMSE (Eq. 6) of 1.795 m/s and an MAE (Eq. 7) of 1.388 m/s.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "MAE of 1.388 m/s",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The cumulative distributions of the absolute deviations between the SAR/CMOD5 and WRF models are shown in Figure 5b. The deviation distribution is concentrated at low values: 44.7% of the absolute deviations are below 1 m/s, more than 75% are below 2m/s, and over 90% remain under 3m/s. Eror metrics [58] indicate a RMSE (Eq. 6) of 1.795 m/s and an MAE (Eq. 7) of 1.388 m/s.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "RMSE of 2.54 m/s",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The wind speed distributions from the WRF model and the Itaja\u00ed buoy are compared in Figure 8. Panel (a) shows the relative frequency of the wind speed values binned at 2 m/s intervals, revealing distinct distributions-most notably, a heavier right tail in the buoy data. Despite these differences, the cumulative distribution of absolute deviations in panel (b) indicates that more than 80% of the absolute deviations fall below 3 m/s, while over 60% are below 2 m/s and more than 30% are below 1 m/s. The calculated RMSE and MAE are 2.54 m/s and 1.96 m/s, respectively.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model",
